# This is a template for the henplus.e2e end-to-end test suite
# (E2EConnectionsIT, E2EDiscoveryIT, E2EDestructiveIT) that connects to real
# databases to confirm the JDBC drivers declared in pom.xml actually work.
# It's a template, not the real thing: it lives in this repository under
# version control, so it must never contain real credentials.
#
# To use it:
#
#   1. Copy it to where E2EConnectionsIT actually looks for it:
#
#        mkdir -p ~/.config/henplus
#        cp src/test/resources/e2e-connectstrings.properties.example \
#           ~/.config/henplus/e2e-connectstrings.properties
#
#   2. Uncomment the three lines (".url", ".username", ".password") for
#      whichever database(s) you want to test against, and fill in real
#      values. Leave every other database's lines commented out or removed -
#      only databases with a ".url" line get tested.
#
#   3. Run the end-to-end test suite:
#
#        mvn verify
#
#      (a plain "mvn package" or "mvn test" never touches this file or the
#      network - only "mvn verify" runs *IT.java tests). With nothing
#      uncommented, or with this file entirely absent, "mvn verify" still
#      succeeds - it just prints a warning and skips.
#
# Format: for each target, "<name>.url" is required; "<name>.username" and
# "<name>.password" are optional (e.g. SQLite needs neither). <name> can be
# anything - it's just a label, shown as the test name in the report.
#
# --- Destructive testing (E2EDestructiveIT) ---
#
# E2EDestructiveIT runs a real create table/index/view -> insert -> dump-out
# -> delete -> dump-in -> count -> drop round trip through henplus's own
# dump-out/dump-in commands. It only runs against a target if BOTH of these
# are true - either one missing skips it cleanly, no build failure:
#
#   1. That target's ".url" is connected to a database literally named
#      "e2e-destructive" - checked live via the driver's own
#      Connection.getCatalog(), not string-parsed from the URL. This is NOT
#      created for you: connecting to a database that doesn't exist yet
#      fails before any test code runs, and "CREATE DATABASE" isn't portable
#      enough across drivers to automate (Oracle has no real equivalent;
#      SQLite's file just gets created automatically, so this doesn't apply
#      to it at all). Create it by hand once, e.g. for Postgres:
#
#        CREATE DATABASE "e2e-destructive";
#
#      (the quotes are required - "e2e-destructive" has a hyphen, which
#      isn't a valid unquoted SQL identifier character).
#
#   2. "<name>.destructive=true" is set for that target - a second,
#      independent opt-in, so a correctly-named database alone is never
#      enough by accident.
#
# e.g.:
#
#   postgres_destructive.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/e2e-destructive
#   postgres_destructive.username=postgres
#   postgres_destructive.password=secret
#   postgres_destructive.destructive=true

# PostgreSQL - https://jdbc.postgresql.org/documentation/use/#connection-parameters
# NOTE: a Postgres connection is scoped to exactly one *database* (the last
# path segment of the URL) - you can't query across databases on the same
# server from a single connection, unlike a schema, which the same
# connection can see plenty of. If your Postgres server hosts several
# databases you want covered, add one target per database, e.g.:
#   postgres.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/postgres
#   myapp.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/myapp
#
#postgres.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/postgres
#postgres.username=postgres
#postgres.password=
#psql_destructive.url=jdbc:postgresql://....
#psql_destructive.username=postgres
#psql_destructive.password=REDACTED


# MySQL - https://dev.mysql.com/doc/connector-j/en/connector-j-reference-jdbc-url-format.html
#mysql.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/test
#mysql.username=root
#mysql.password=

# MariaDB - https://mariadb.com/kb/en/about-mariadb-connector-j/#connection-strings
#mariadb.url=jdbc:mariadb://localhost:3306/test
#mariadb.username=root
#mariadb.password=

# Microsoft SQL Server - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/connect/jdbc/building-the-connection-url
#mssql.url=jdbc:sqlserver://localhost:1433;databaseName=test;encrypt=false
#mssql.username=sa
#mssql.password=

# Oracle - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/jjdbc/data-sources-and-URLs.html
# (thin-style URL; swap the SID for a service name with @//host:port/service if needed)
#oracle.url=jdbc:oracle:thin:@localhost:1521:xe
#oracle.username=system
#oracle.password=

# IBM Db2 - https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/db2/11.5?topic=cdsudidsdjs-data-server-driver-jdbc-sqlj-types-4-connectivity
#db2.url=jdbc:db2://localhost:50000/testdb
#db2.username=db2inst1
#db2.password=

# SQLite - https://github.com/xerial/sqlite-jdbc#usage - file-based, no server, no credentials needed
#sqlite.url=jdbc:sqlite:/tmp/henplus-e2e-test.db

# --- "oddball" drivers, same portability spirit as the rest of this roster ---

# Vertica - https://docs.vertica.com/latest/en/client-drivers/jdbc/
#vertica.url=jdbc:vertica://localhost:5433/vmart
#vertica.username=dbadmin
#vertica.password=

# H2 - embedded, zero external infra, self-testable with no server at all -
# https://www.h2database.com/html/features.html#database_url
#h2.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
#h2.username=sa
#h2.password=

# DuckDB - embedded analytical (OLAP) engine, also zero external infra -
# https://duckdb.org/docs/api/java
#duckdb.url=jdbc:duckdb:/tmp/henplus-e2e-test.duckdb

# ClickHouse - https://clickhouse.com/docs/en/integrations/java#jdbc-driver
#clickhouse.url=jdbc:clickhouse://localhost:8123/default
#clickhouse.username=default
#clickhouse.password=

# Firebird - https://firebirdsql.org/file/documentation/drivers_documentation/java/faq.html
#firebird.url=jdbc:firebirdsql://localhost:3050/path/to/database.fdb
#firebird.username=sysdba
#firebird.password=
